{"type":"message","id":"d9b56a11","parentId":"5fe5254e","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:36:35.335Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_OdLc3m4TK4UNcRg1IqhPI355","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"ok\",\n  \"story_id\": \"us-arma-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-2026-05-22\",\n  \"topic_theme\": \"US lawmakers move to codify a strategic Bitcoin reserve through the American Reserve Modernization Act\",\n  \"primary_keyword\": \"Strategic Bitcoin reserve\",\n  \"primary_headline\": \"US lawmakers push ARMA bill to lock a strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law\",\n  \"primary_asset\": \"Bitcoin\",\n  \"chart_coin\": \"bitcoin\",\n  \"sources_used\": [\n    \"Decrypt\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph\",\n    \"CryptoSlate\",\n    \"CryptoFox\"\n  ],\n  \"source_urls\": [\n    \"https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\",\n    \"https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans\",\n    \"https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\",\n    \"https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve\"\n  ],\n  \"combined_key_facts\": [\n    \"Representative Nick Begich introduced the American Reserve Modernization Act, with Democrat Jared Golden serving as the primary co-lead, to put a US strategic Bitcoin reserve into statute rather than leave it to executive discretion.\",\n    \"The proposal would direct the Treasury Department to create and maintain a Bitcoin reserve for at least 20 years, while also establishing a separate Digital Asset Stockpile for federally held non-Bitcoin crypto assets.\",\n    \"Cross-referenced reporting says the reserve would consolidate crypto already held across federal agencies, much of it originating from seizures, forfeitures and penalties.\",\n    \"CoinTelegraph and CryptoSlate both reported the US currently holds about 328,372 BTC, worth roughly $25 billion to $25.5 billion, making it the largest nation-state Bitcoin holder.\",\n    \"The bill includes transparency measures such as public proof-of-reserve reporting, independent third-party audits and direct congressional oversight of the reserve.\",\n    \"Coverage also highlights a budget-neutral growth concept for the reserve, while explicitly protecting Americans\\u2019 lawful rights to own, transfer and self-custody digital assets.\"\n  ],\n  \"aggregated_raw_content\": \"\\n\\nSOURCE: Decrypt\\nURL: https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\\n\\n#### In brief\\n\\n- Reps. Nick Begich (R-AK) and Jared Golden (D-ME) introduced the American Reserve Modernization Act.\\n- The bill seeks formally codify a strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law, which would be managed for at least 20 years.\\n- Aimed at cementing a core Trump administration promise, the initiative has faced ongoing hurdles.\\n\\nRep. Nick Begich (R-AK) introduced legislation on Thursday aimed at enshrining a strategic reserve for Bitcoin in federal law, seeking to cement one of President Donald Trump\\u2019s core campaign promises for digital asset holders ahead of the high-stakes U.S. midterm elections.\\n\\nThe American Reserve Modernization Act, or ARMA, would direct the Treasury Department to create and maintain a reserve of the digital asset for a minimum of 20 years, while establishing a stockpile for other cryptocurrencies held by the government.\\n\\nThe legislation also mandates the consolidation of what is believed to be billions of dollars in digital assets held across federal agencies\\u2014attained through actions like forfeitures and penalties\\u2014while providing transparency through a proof-of-reserve report.\\n\\nAs of last month, the strategic reserve for Bitcoin that Trump created via executive order is still a work in progress. At a conference in Las Vegas, Patrick Witt, executive director of the President\\u2019s Council of Advisors for Digital Assets, said the current administration has spent months \\u201cfiguring out\\u201d the legal interpretations associated with the initiative.\\n\\nAmerica\\u2019s reserves balance sheet is a critical component of our nation\\u2019s insurance policy, bolstering our currency and providing assurance during times of uncertainty. Over time, the prevailing sentiment as to what constitutes a durable store of value can shift, and as such it is\\u2026 pic.twitter.com/7XfBwAWFGi\\n\\n\\u2014 Congressman Nick Begich (@RepNickBegich) May 21, 2026\\n\\nJoining Begich as the bill\\u2019s primary Democratic co-lead is Rep. Jared Golden (D-ME). The ARMA Act meanwhile counts 17 original co-sponsors, including Rep. Mike Collins (R-GA), among the first lawmakers to disclose an investment in a meme coin on Capitol Hill.\\n\\n\\u201cAdministrations have auctioned [crypto] off or held it in reserve, according to the whims of the executive branch,\\u201d Golden said in a statement, noting that a stockpile created under the ARMA Act would enjoy the \\u201cweight of law.\\u201d\\n\\nAlthough a former administration official had said that the White House was eager to purchase as much Bitcoin as possible in a \\u201cbudget-neutral way,\\u201d Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent later dented enthusiasm by ruling out any agency purchases.\\n\\nThe bill is aimed at providing the flexibility needed to broaden America\\u2019s portfolio of reserve assets that effectively serve as insurance policy, while also safeguarding crypto from \\u201cthe whims of Congress or future administrations,\\u201d Begich said in an X post on Thursday.\\n\\nEstablished in May 2025 with a stroke of the president\\u2019s pen, the strategic reserve for Bitcoin was swiftly skewered by critics, including Rep. Maxine Waters (D-CA), the ranking member of the House Financial Services Committee, who warned of benefits for Trump insiders.\\n\\n\\u201cTypically, when the government holds strategic reserves, it is for an essential input that powers the U.S. economy and day-to-day life for American families,\\u201d she said. \\u201cCrypto, however, does not fall into these categories, because it has no inherent value.\\u201d\\n\\nAlthough the much-hyped stockpile\\u2019s creation represented a symbolic win for the digital assets industry, the government has yet to announce any allocations. Last year, Sen. Cynthia Lummis (R-WY) re-introduced the BITCOIN Act, a bill that directs the Treasury to purchase 1 million Bitcoin over five years in a way that seeks to avoid taxing citizens.\\n\\n*Decrypt* has reached out to Begich and Golden for comment.\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nSOURCE: CoinTelegraph\\nURL: https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans\\n\\nIn an interview on Sunday, Patrick Witt, of the President\\u2019s Council of Advisors for Digital Assets, referred to ARMA as \\u201cVersion 2\\u201d of the BITCOIN Act and said the White House has spent considerable time examining the legal implications of a Bitcoin reserve.\\n\\n\\u201cIt's a breakthrough as far as getting everything in place, legally sound, properly safeguarding the assets.\\u201d\\n\\nThe push for a federal policy comes as the US currently holds 328,372 Bitcoin worth more than $25.5 billion \\u2014 the most of any nation-state \\u2014 but has sold portions of those holdings through court-ordered actions over the years.\\n\\n\\u201cThe US is already one of the largest holders of Bitcoin in the world. But Congress has never set a federal policy on what to do with that asset,\\u201d said US Representative Jared Golden, one of the 16 co-sponsors of the bill.\\n\\nUnder ARMA, Bitcoin must be held for a minimum of 20 years unless it is sold to reduce America\\u2019s national debt, which topped $39 trillion on Wednesday.\\n\\nLike the BITCOIN Act, ARMA also seeks to acquire up to 1 million Bitcoin over five years through budget-neutral strategies, meaning it would avoid using taxpayer money.\\n\\nUS Representative Mike Carey argued that as digital assets continue to grow in importance, the bill could strengthen America\\u2019s long-term economic position and help keep it \\u201ccompetitive on the world stage.\\u201d\\n\\n**Related: ****SpaceX reveals larger-than-expected Bitcoin holdings in IPO filing**** **\\n\\nStrive CEO and chairman Matt Cole said ARMA is the \\u201csingle most important crypto legislation\\u201d that could come out of Washington DC.\\n\\nQuarterly proof of reserve reports and independent third-party audits of the Bitcoin reserve would be published under ARMA, Begich noted.\\n\\nThe bill also seeks to protect digital property rights by affirming that the federal government may not impair the right of individuals to own or self-custody digital assets.\\n\\n**Magazine: ****eToro founder timed Bitcoin top perfectly due to belief in 4 year cycles**\\n\\nMore on the subject\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nSOURCE: CryptoSlate\\nURL: https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\\n\\n# US lawmakers push new Strategic Bitcoin Reserve act to secure $25 billion federal stash\\n\\nARMA would force the government to hold its Bitcoin for two decades to build a strategic financial buffer.\\n\\nA renewed push to solidify the United States\\u2019 foothold in the digital asset market emerged Thursday as lawmakers unveiled legislation to establish a formal strategic Bitcoin reserve and consolidate the federal government's multibillion-dollar cryptocurrency stockpile.\\n\\nSpearheaded by Representative Nick Begich, the American Reserve Modernization Act (ARMA) of 2026 mandates the creation of a secure Bitcoin reserve within the US Department of the Treasury, alongside a separate \\u201cDigital Asset Stockpile\\u201d for federally held, non-Bitcoin cryptocurrencies.\\n\\nThe legislation aims to provide a long-term structure to the government\\u2019s existing digital wealth, primarily amassed through law enforcement seizures. According to data from Bitcoin Treasuries, the US currently holds 328,372 Bitcoin, a stash valued at more than $25 billion.\\n\\nBegich said in a statement:\\n\\n\\u201cThe American Reserve Modernization Act positions the United States to lead confidently in the digital age while protecting taxpayer interests, strengthening financial sovereignty, and reinforcing the principles of transparency and sound stewardship.\\u201d\\n\\nThe bill has 17 original House co-sponsors, including Buddy Carter, Ben Cline of Virginia, Barry Moore of Alabama, Burgess Owens of Utah, Mariannette Miller-Meeks of Iowa, Mike Carey and Michael Rulli of Ohio, Mike Collins of Georgia, Mike Lawler of New York, Riley Moore of West Virginia, and Tim Moore of North Carolina.\\n\\nApart from the strong legislative support, crypto industry leaders are already throwing their weight behind the measure.\\n\\nMatt Cole, CEO of Bitcoin treasury firm Strive, called the proposal \\u201cthe single most important crypto legislation that can come out of D.C.\\u201d for the long-term health and security of the United States.\\n\\n## ARMA establishes a 20-year Bitcoin bet\\n\\nIf enacted, the bill would radically alter how Washington handles digital assets. Rather than periodically auctioning off seized crypto, the Treasury would be forced to consolidate custody and oversight across all federal agencies.\\n\\nCrucially, the legislation requires that any Bitcoin held in the strategic reserve be maintained for at least 20 years, effectively turning the US government into a long-term \\u201chodler.\\u201d\\n\\nTo address persistent industry demands for transparency, the bill establishes stringent reporting measures. The Treasury would be required to publish quarterly public \\u201cProof of Reserve\\u201d reports, submit to independent third-party audits, and face direct congressional oversight.\\n\\nThe legislation also directs a study into budget-neutral acquisition strategies. The goal is to evaluate lawful methods for the government to expand its digital reserves without relying on tax increases, deficit spending, or adding to the national debt.\n\n⚠️ [... middle content omitted — showing head and tail ...]\n\n structure that would make the proposal a direct bet on converting part of the federal balance sheet into Bitcoin exposure.\\n\\nLummis legislation followed President Donald Trump's executive order to establish a strategic Bitcoin reserve.\\n\\nHowever, these initiatives have considerably stalled despite the Trump administration's pro-crypto stance.\\n\\nStill, the Begich bill aims to build on the recent advancement of the Clarity Act, which passed the Senate Banking Committee with bipartisan support. That bill is designed to establish clear rules of the road for the industry and encourage institutional investment.\\n\\nBitcoin is **-0.82%** over the past 24 hours and currently sits at rank #1 by market cap.\\n\\n## Where the broader market sits right now\\n\\nRight now, the total crypto market is valued at **$2.59T** with **$76.67B** in 24-hour volume. Bitcoin dominance sits at **59.97%**. Explore the market\\n\\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\\n\\nSOURCE: CryptoFox\\nURL: https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve\\n\\nUS Representative **Nick Begich** has introduced the **Reserve Modernization Act**, a legislative proposal designed to formalize the role of digital assets within the national treasury. Supported by **16 original co-sponsors**, the bill seeks to establish a permanent **strategic Bitcoin reserve**, integrating federally held digital assets into a long-term storage framework. This initiative marks a significant shift in federal fiscal policy, aiming to treat **BTC** as a cornerstone of the nation's financial security for the coming decades.\\n\\n## Legislative Framework and 20-Year Lock-In Period\\n\\nThe proposed act focuses on the long-term preservation of digital assets. Under the terms of the legislation, any Bitcoin held within the strategic reserve would be **locked for a minimum period of 20 years**. This provision is intended to prevent short-term liquidation and ensure that the asset serves as a generational store of value. *By mandating a two-decade holding period, the bill aims to insulate the reserve from market volatility and shifting political priorities.*\\n\\nThe bill also outlines strategies for the **integration of existing federally held digital assets**, which often originate from law enforcement seizures. Rather than auctioning these assets, the government would transition them into the sovereign reserve.\\n\\n## Budget Neutrality and Legal Protections\\n\\nA key component of the Reserve Modernization Act is its focus on **budget-neutral acquisition strategies**. Lawmakers are exploring methods to expand the Bitcoin holdings without increasing the national deficit or requiring new taxpayer funding. Furthermore, the bill includes specific legal safeguards to:\\n\\n- Protect the reserve from\\n**future liquidation efforts**by subsequent administrations. - Establish clear jurisdictional oversight for the management of the\\n**blockchain-based assets**. - Codify the reserve into\\n**official law**, providing a more permanent foundation than previous executive orders.\\n\\n*While prior administrative actions had moved toward creating a reserve through executive authority, this legislative approach seeks to provide a statutory mandate that would be significantly harder to reverse.*\\n\\nIn conclusion, the introduction of the Reserve Modernization Act on **May 22, 2026**, represents a major step toward the institutionalization of cryptocurrency at the federal level. By proposing a mandatory holding period and budget-neutral growth, the bill aims to stabilize the national balance sheet through the inclusion of decentralized assets. The progress of this legislation will be closely monitored by global markets as an indicator of the United States' long-term stance on **digital finance** and sovereign asset management.\\n\\n## Frequently Asked *Questions*\\n\\nQuick answers to the most common questions about this topic.\",\n  \"tweet_quotes\": []\n}[... 693 more characters truncated]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779438995335}}
{"type":"message","id":"2c7d6349","parentId":"d9b56a11","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:36:35.335Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_B7g4tublxROoKEvupMqwmDDN","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"{\n  \"status\": \"error\",\n  \"tool\": \"read\",\n  \"error\": \"ENOENT: no such file or directory, access '/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-083045/COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md'\"\n}"}],"details":{"status":"error","tool":"read","error":"ENOENT: no such file or directory, access '/tmp/crypto-run-20260522-083045/COINOGRAPHY_TEMPLATE.md'"},"isError":false,"timestamp":1779438995335}}
{"type":"message","id":"77167975","parentId":"2c7d6349","timestamp":"2026-05-22T08:36:35.336Z","message":{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_BqMqwZ6NL7z5RgWjlnt4tDdG","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"META\n- SEO Title: Strategic Bitcoin reserve bill targets $25B\n- Meta Description: US lawmakers want to codify a strategic Bitcoin reserve, lock federal BTC for 20 years, and publish audits on roughly $25 billion in holdings.\n- URL Slug: strategic-bitcoin-reserve-bill\n- Categories: Regulation, Latest News\n- Primary Keyword: Strategic Bitcoin reserve\n- Secondary Keywords: ARMA bill, Bitcoin reserve, US Treasury, self-custody rights\n\n---\n\n# Strategic Bitcoin reserve bill targets $25B federal stash\n\nThe latest push for a Strategic Bitcoin reserve is moving from campaign slogan to drafted law. House lawmakers introduced the American Reserve Modernization Act, or ARMA, to put federal Bitcoin policy on a statutory footing, according to early reporting from [Decrypt](https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law). That matters because executive actions can shift with a new administration, while legislation sets a harder baseline for how seized and held digital assets are managed.\n\nThe proposal arrives with a concrete number behind it. The US is already reported to control about 328,372 BTC, worth roughly $25 billion to $25.5 billion, and [CoinTelegraph](https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans) says that stash remains the largest known nation-state Bitcoin position. For traders, that makes this less about a symbolic headline and more about whether Washington is preparing to treat Bitcoin as a reserve asset instead of an inventory item for future auctions.\n\nNick Begich introduced the bill, with Democrat Jared Golden serving as the primary co-lead. That bipartisan framing does not guarantee passage, but it does make the story more relevant than a single-party messaging exercise. If ARMA gains traction, the market will start pricing a lower probability that federal Bitcoin gets sold into future strength and a higher probability that the government becomes a structurally patient holder.\n\n## Why ARMA changes the policy baseline\n\n### From executive action to statute\n\nThe core shift in ARMA is legal durability. Existing reserve efforts have largely sat in the orbit of executive authority, which leaves room for policy reversal, uneven implementation, or simple delays while agencies work through custody and compliance questions. ARMA attempts to answer that by putting the Strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law and assigning the Treasury Department a direct management role.\n\nThat move is aimed at two recurring problems. First, agencies have not followed a single long-term rulebook for crypto that enters federal hands through seizures, forfeitures, and penalties. Second, the market has had no stable signal on whether those holdings would be auctioned, retained, or reorganized under a broader reserve framework.\n\nA statute would not erase politics, but it would raise the cost of reversing course. It would also give traders a cleaner way to assess future supply risk. If a large federal stash is effectively fenced off by law, even without fresh purchases, the market can treat that BTC as less liquid than it appeared during prior auction-heavy periods.\n\n### Why the bipartisan angle matters\n\nGolden's role is a key detail because it broadens the bill's credibility beyond a purely pro-crypto Republican talking point. Congress still has to navigate committee work, competing legislative priorities, and criticism from lawmakers who do not view Bitcoin as a strategic national asset. Even so, bipartisan sponsorship tends to extend a bill's shelf life and improve its odds of surviving changes in political momentum.\n\nFor markets, this does not mean imminent enactment. It means the odds of repeated discussion, hearings, amendments, and institutional attention just moved higher. Bitcoin often reacts not only to passed laws, but also to signals that large pools of capital or sovereign actors may adopt a longer time horizon.\n\n## What sits inside the reserve plan\n\n### The 20-year lock and the federal crypto split\n\nARMA would require the Treasury to maintain a Bitcoin reserve for at least 20 years, while also creating a separate Digital Asset Stockpile for non-Bitcoin cryptocurrencies already held by the government. That distinction is important. It frames Bitcoin as the core reserve asset and pushes other tokens into a different policy bucket, rather than treating every seized digital asset as equal.\n\nThe bill's reported design appears to do four things at once:\n\n- Consolidate digital assets scattered across federal agencies into a more unified custody structure.\n- Ring-fence Bitcoin for long-duration holding instead of routine liquidation.\n- Separate non-Bitcoin assets into a stockpile with its own management track.\n- Build public reporting standards that markets can monitor over time.\n\nThis is a meaningful change in how federal inventory might be classified. Instead of seeing government-held BTC as dormant supply that can return to market whenever policy changes, ARMA would recast it as a balance sheet asset with a strategic role. That framing could influence how traders think about long-term sell pressure, especially during future bull cycles when government disposals would otherwise attract outsized attention.\n\nThe proposal also keeps alive a budget-neutral growth idea. Cross-referenced reporting suggests lawmakers want the option to expand holdings without new taxpayer funding, tax increases, or a larger deficit. That is still a concept rather than a completed acquisition program, but the point is clear: supporters want reserve growth to look like balance sheet engineering, not a politically toxic spending initiative.\n\n## Market implications for Bitcoin and policy traders\n\n### Transparency, debt optics, and self-custody rights\n\nARMA is not just a hold-the-Bitcoin bill. It reportedly includes quarterly proof-of-reserve reporting, independent third-party audits, and direct congressional oversight. If those requirements survive the legislative process, they could create one of the clearest public reporting regimes attached to any sovereign Bitcoin reserve proposal.\n\nThat matters because opacity has been a constant problem around official crypto holdings. Traders can estimate wallet balances and trace some movements, but they rarely get a clean institutional framework for custody, reporting cadence, and disposal rules. A proof-of-reserve model would not remove every uncertainty, though it would sharply improve visibility around one of the market's largest publicly discussed sovereign positions.\n\nThere is also a debt angle. Some reporting says Bitcoin in the reserve could be sold to reduce the national debt, which means the 20-year hold is strong but not absolute. Traders should read that as a conditional floor under federal holding behavior, not an ironclad ban on future sales. If debt reduction becomes the stated rationale for liquidation, Washington would still retain a path to unlock supply.\n\nThe bill's self-custody language is another market signal worth watching. By affirming the lawful right of Americans to own, transfer, and self-custody digital assets, ARMA ties reserve policy to a broader property-rights stance. That combination could help shape sentiment around future market structure debates, especially if lawmakers begin bundling sovereign Bitcoin policy with retail ownership protections.\n\n## Conclusion\n\nThe Strategic Bitcoin reserve story just became more concrete because ARMA tries to move federal Bitcoin policy out of executive improvisation and into law. With roughly 328,372 BTC already under US control, the main issue for traders is not whether the government can influence supply, but whether it chooses to act like a seller, a custodian, or a strategic holder. Until the bill's path is clearer, the cleanest read is to track committee progress, audit language, and any shift in the budget-neutral purchase discussion, because those details will shape how much sovereign conviction the market prices into Bitcoin.\n\n## FAQs\n\n**1. What is the ARMA bill trying to do?**\n\nARMA would put a Strategic Bitcoin reserve into federal law and assign the Treasury Department responsibility for maintaining it. The proposal would also create a separate Digital Asset Stockpile for federally held crypto assets other than Bitcoin. The main goal is to replace ad hoc handling of seized digital assets with a formal long-term framework.\n\n**2. How much Bitcoin does the US government reportedly hold now?**\n\nCross-referenced coverage puts the current figure at about 328,372 BTC. At recent prices, that equates to roughly $25 billion to $25.5 billion. That makes the United States the largest known nation-state Bitcoin holder in current reporting.\n\n**3. Does the bill mean the government will buy more Bitcoin right away?**\n\nNo, not right away. The reporting points to a budget-neutral acquisition concept and a study of lawful ways to expand holdings without taxpayer funding, but that is different from an immediate purchase order. Traders should separate the existing stash from any future accumulation plan until the legislative text advances further.\n\n**4. Why are audits and proof-of-reserve reports important here?**\n\nThey would give the market a clearer view of what the government holds, how those assets are custodied, and whether balances are changing. That reduces uncertainty around a very large sovereign Bitcoin position. Better transparency can matter for pricing because traders spend less time guessing about potential supply shocks.\n\n**5. Could the reserve still be sold in the future?**\n\nYes, based on current reporting, there appears to be an exception tied to reducing the national debt. That means the reserve would be designed for long-duration holding, but not under every possible circumstance. The difference is that sales would move from discretionary habit to a narrower, more defined policy exception.\n\n**Sources:**\n- Decrypt: https://decrypt.co/368662/proposed-arma-bill-aims-enshrine-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-law\n- CoinTelegraph: https://cointelegraph.com/news/us-senators-unveil-new-bitcoin-bill-renewing-strategic-reserve-plans\n- CryptoSlate: https://cryptoslate.com/us-lawmakers-push-new-strategic-bitcoin-reserve-act-to-secure-25-billion-federal-stash/\n- CryptoFox: https://cryptofox.news/news/us-lawmakers-introduce-bill-to-establish-strategic-bitcoin-reserve\n\n[Word Count: 1109]\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1779438995335}}
